WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:10.753
Why are there exactly 435 members of the&nbsp;
House of Representatives? It's not set by

00:00:10.753 --> 00:00:17.600
the Constitution, but the Permanent Apportionment Act&nbsp;
of 1929. Back then, there were fewer than 250,000

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:23.440
people per district. Today, there are nearly three&nbsp;
quarters of a million. So, it's fair to ask just how

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:28.640
representative the House of Representatives&nbsp;
actually is. James Madison, the father of the

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:33.520
Constitution, would be dubious. The House was to&nbsp;
be where the Representatives of America, in all

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:39.626
its diversity, would come together. But can&nbsp;
one person really speak for 750,000?

00:00:39.626 --> 00:00:44.880
Today's average district is larger than any state back&nbsp;
when the Constitution was ratified. So, what can

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:50.800
we do about that? Madison might suggest: increase&nbsp;
the size of the House. In Federalist 56, he uses

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:56.480
the British House of Commons as a model for the&nbsp;
size of the House. Today, the Commons has 650 seats

00:00:56.480 --> 00:01:01.680
and works reasonably well. So, maybe we could add&nbsp;
200 more seats to the house without undermining

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:07.125
its ability to legislate. That would make our&nbsp;
House and our government more representative.

